data.content:
The lightest approach depends on your target framework:

### .NET 8+ (recommended): use the built-in `TimeProvider`

`TimeProvider` is an abstract class in the BCL — no packages, no DI container needed. Add a defaulted parameter:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-30)); // can rewind too
```

### Pre-.NET 8: ambient `Func`

Zero interfaces, zero containers:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    internal static Func<DateTimeOffset> UtcNow = () => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate) => UtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

Tests just swap the func and restore it in `Dispose`.

---

**Go with `TimeProvider`** if you're on .NET 8+. It's a first-party abstraction, needs no DI wiring, and `FakeTimeProvider` gives you `Advance()` / `SetUtcNow()` out of the box. Want me to apply the change to your file?
